{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Rescuers Down Under is a 1990 American animated comedy-drama adventure film produced by Walt Disney Feature Animation and released by Walt Disney Pictures on November 16, 1990.","title":"The Rescuers Down Under"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The 29th Disney animated feature film, the film is the sequel to the 1977 animated film "The Rescuers", which was based on the novels of Margery Sharp.","title":"The Rescuers Down Under"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Set in the Australian Outback, the film centers on Bernard and Bianca traveling to Australia to save a boy named Cody from a villainous poacher in pursuit of an endangered bird of prey.","title":"The Rescuers Down Under"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Muppets: An Original Walt Disney Records Soundtrack is a soundtrack album released by Walt Disney Records on November 22, 2011 for the musical comedy film "The Muppets".","title":"The Muppets (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The soundtrack features five original songs, four re-recordings and remasterings of popular Muppet songs ("The Muppet Show Theme", "Rainbow Connection", and "Mah Nà Mah Nà"), two cover versions of existing songs (Cee Lo Green's "Forget You" and Nirvana's "Smells Like Teen Spirit"), two standalone songs (Paul Simon's "Me and Julio Down by the Schoolyard" and Starship's "We Built This City"), and fifteen dialogue tracks.","title":"The Muppets (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It also features the song "Man or Muppet", which won the Academy Award for Best Original Song.","title":"The Muppets (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The soundtrack was also nominated for Best Compilation Soundtrack for Visual Media at the 55th Grammy Awards.","title":"The Muppets (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"In the history of The Walt Disney Company, the Disney Renaissance refers to the era from 1989 to 1999 during which Walt Disney Feature Animation (renamed Walt Disney Animation Studios in 2006) experienced a creative resurgence in producing successful animated films based on well-known stories, which restored public and critical interest in The Walt Disney Company as a whole.","title":"Disney Renaissance"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" During this era, the studio produced and released ten animated films: "The Little Mermaid" (1989), "The Rescuers Down Under" (1990), "Beauty and the Beast" (1991), "Aladdin" (1992), "The Lion King" (1994), "Pocahontas" (1995), "The Hunchback of Notre Dame" (1996), "Hercules" (1997), "Mulan" (1998) and "Tarzan" (1999).","title":"Disney Renaissance"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Eva Gabor ( ; February 11, 1919 – July 4, 1995) was a Hungarian-born American actress, comedian, singer and socialite.","title":"Eva Gabor"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She was widely known for her role on the 1965–71 television sitcom "Green Acres" as Lisa Douglas, the wife of Eddie Albert's character, Oliver Wendell Douglas.","title":"Eva Gabor"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She voiced "Duchess" in the 1970 Disney film "The Aristocats", and Miss Bianca in Disney’s "The Rescuers" and "The Rescuers Down Under".","title":"Eva Gabor"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Gabor was successful as an actress in film, on Broadway and on television.","title":"Eva Gabor"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" She was also a successful businessperson, marketing wigs, clothing and beauty products.","title":"Eva Gabor"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Her elder sisters, Zsa Zsa and Magda Gabor, were also actresses and socialites.","title":"Eva Gabor"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"William Paul "Bill" Barretta (born June 19, 1964) is an American puppeteer and producer who has been performing with The Muppets since 1991, when he puppeteered the body of Sinclair family patriarch, Earl Sinclair on "Dinosaurs".","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He later developed several new characters on "Muppets Tonight", including Pepe the King Prawn, Johnny Fiama, Big Mean Carl and Bobo the Bear.","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Along with having his own Muppet characters, Barretta has taken over several of Jim Henson's roles, such as Dr. Teeth, Rowlf the Dog, Mahna Mahna and Swedish Chef, and briefly took over Jerry Nelson's role of Lew Zealand.","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" His film debut as a principal puppeteer was in 1996's "Muppet Treasure Island" as Clueless Morgan.","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In addition, Barretta has produced two of the Muppets' television films, "It's a Very Merry Muppet Christmas Movie" (2002) and "The Muppets' Wizard of Oz" (2005).","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Barretta also provides additional voices on "Kim Possible".","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" His most recent film performance was in Disney's "Muppets Most Wanted", where he also served as a co-producer.","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Barretta also served as an executive producer on the ABC series, "The Muppets".","title":"Bill Barretta"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Muppets Most Wanted: An Original Walt Disney Records Soundtrack is a soundtrack album released by Walt Disney Records on March 18, 2014 for the musical comedy film "Muppets Most Wanted".","title":"Muppets Most Wanted (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The soundtrack features six original songs, two re-recordings of popular Muppet songs ("The Muppet Show Theme" and "Together Again"), three cover versions of existing songs (Allen Toussaint's "Working in the Coal Mine", Maroon 5's "Moves Like Jagger", and Los del Río's "Macarena (Bayside Boys Mix)"), an orchestral suite by Christophe Beck, five demos by Bret McKenzie, and eight dialogue tracks.","title":"Muppets Most Wanted (soundtrack)"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: The Rescuers
