{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lafayette College is a private liberal arts college based in Easton, Pennsylvania, with a campus in New York City, NY.","title":"Lafayette College"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The school, founded in 1826 by James Madison Porter, son of General Andrew Porter of Norristown, and the citizens of Easton, first began holding classes in 1832.","title":"Lafayette College"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The founders voted to name the school after General Lafayette, who famously toured the country in 1824–25, as "a testimony of respect for [his] talents, virtues, and signal services... in the great cause of freedom".","title":"Lafayette College"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Southern Methodist University (SMU) is a private research university in Dallas, University Park, and Highland Park, Texas.","title":"Southern Methodist University"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Founded in 1911 by the Methodist Episcopal Church, South, SMU operates satellite campuses in Plano, Texas, and Taos, New Mexico.","title":"Southern Methodist University"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" SMU is owned by the South Central Jurisdiction of the United Methodist Church.","title":"Southern Methodist University"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Of the university's 11,643 students, 6,411 are undergraduates.","title":"Southern Methodist University"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Dr. June Schlueter is Charles A. Dana Professor Emerita of English at Lafayette College in Easton, Pennsylvania.","title":"June Schlueter"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Her areas of specialty are Shakespeare, Early Modern England, and Modern Drama.","title":"June Schlueter"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She is married to Dr. Paul Schlueter, who is a specialist in English literature.","title":"June Schlueter"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Dr. Schlueter has taught at Lafayette College since 1977 where she has taught: Modern Drama (American, British, Comparative); Shakespeare; The London Theatre (in London); Drama Survey (Greeks through Shakespeare, Restoration through Modern); Tudor and Stuart Drama; introductory writing and literature courses; British literature survey; Major American Writers; introductory writing and literature courses; interdisciplinary courses on the McCarthy era and on literature, science, and technology; advised student dramatic productions.","title":"June Schlueter"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" From 1993 until 2006 she was Provost of Lafayette College.","title":"June Schlueter"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Dr. Schlueter has served on the Board of Trustees for Fairleigh Dickinson University.","title":"June Schlueter"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Madison A. "Matty" Bell (February 22, 1899 – June 30, 1983) was an American football player, coach of football and basketball, and college athletics administrator.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He played for Centre, captain of its 1918 team.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He served as the head football coach at the Haskell Institute (1920–1921), Carroll College in Waukesha, Wisconsin (1922), Texas Christian University (1923–1928), Texas A&M University (1929–1933), and Southern Methodist University (1935–1941, 1945–1949), compiling a career college football record of 143–87–16.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" His 1935 SMU Mustangs, which have been recognized as a national champion, went 12–0 in the regular season before losing to Stanford in the Rose Bowl.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Bell was also the head basketball coach at Texas Christian for six seasons from 1923 to 1929, tallying a mark of 71–41.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He was inducted into the College Football Hall of Fame as a coach in 1955.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" After retiring from coaching following the 1949 season, Bell served as the athletic director at Southern Methodist until 1964.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" He died in 1983 in Dallas, Texas.","title":"Matty Bell"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Southern Methodist University Press (or SMU Press) is a university press that is part of Southern Methodist University.","title":"Southern Methodist University Press"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was established in 1937 and is the oldest academic publisher in Texas.","title":"Southern Methodist University Press"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The press releases eight to ten titles each year and is known for its literary fiction.","title":"Southern Methodist University Press"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was scheduled to suspend operations on June 1, 2010.","title":"Southern Methodist University Press"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The provost of SMU announced in 2011 plans to reorganize the press with a smaller budget and different goals.","title":"Southern Methodist University Press"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1990 Southern Methodist University Mustangs football team represented the Southern Methodist University in the 1990 college football season.","title":"1990 SMU Mustangs football team"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" SMU opened with a 44-7 win over Vanderbilt, but struggled the remainder of the season still recovering from the Death penalty.","title":"1990 SMU Mustangs football team"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Mustangs offense scored 197 points while the defense allowed 426 points.","title":"1990 SMU Mustangs football team"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Forrest Gregg, who was retiring after the season, was carried off the field following a loss to Arkansas in the final game.","title":"1990 SMU Mustangs football team"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Yes
